"[1411.4028] A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4028)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4028\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Nov 2014]\nL9: # Title:A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Edward Farhi】, 【6†Jeffrey Goldstone】, 【7†Sam Gutmann】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm, by Edward Farhi and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:We introduce a quantum algorithm that produces approximate solutions for combinatorial optimization problems. The algorithm depends on a positive integer p and the quality of the approximation improves as p is increased. The quantum circuit that implements the algorithm consists of unitary gates whose locality is at most the locality of the objective function whose optimum is sought. The depth of the circuit grows linearly with p times (at worst) the number of constraints.\nL17: If p is fixed, that is, independent of the input size, the algorithm makes use of efficient classical preprocessing. If p grows with the input size a different strategy is proposed. We study the algorithm as applied to MaxCut on regular graphs and analyze its performance on 2-regular and 3-regular graphs for fixed p. For p = 1, on 3-regular graphs the quantum algorithm always finds a cut that is at least 0.6924 times the size of the optimal cut.\nL18: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph)\nL19: Report number:  | MIT-CTP/4610\nL20: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:1411.4028】 [quant-ph]\nL21:    | (or 【11†arXiv:1411.4028v1】 [quant-ph] for this version)\nL22:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1411.4028†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Edward Farhi [【13†view email】]\nL26: [v1] Fri, 14 Nov 2014 19:57:57 UTC (22 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm, by Edward Farhi and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL35:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL36:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL37: \nL38: 【15†view license】\nL39: \nL40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: quant-ph\nL43: \nL44: 【16†< prev】   |   【17†next >】\nL45: \nL46: 【18†new】 | 【19†recent】 | 【20†2014-11】\nL47: ### References & Citations\nL48: \nL49:   * 【21†INSPIRE HEP†inspirehep.net】\nL50:   * 【22†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL51:   * 【23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL52:   * 【24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL53: \nL54: ### 【25†6 blog links】\nL55: \nL56: (【26†what is this?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1709.03489] From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz (https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.03489)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.03489\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Sep 2017 (【5†v1】), last revised 28 Feb 2019 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Stuart Hadfield】, 【7†Zhihui Wang】, 【8†Bryan O'Gorman】, 【9†Eleanor G. Rieffel】, 【10†Davide Venturelli】, 【11†Rupak Biswas】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz, by Stuart Hadfield and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【12†View PDF】 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:The next few years will be exciting as prototype universal quantum processors emerge, enabling implementation of a wider variety of algorithms. Of particular interest are quantum heuristics, which require experimentation on quantum hardware for their evaluation, and which have the potential to significantly expand the breadth of quantum computing applications.\nL17: A leading candidate is Farhi et al.'s Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm, which alternates between applying a cost-function-based Hamiltonian and a mixing Hamiltonian. Here, we extend this framework to allow alternation between more general families of operators.\nL18: The essence of this extension, the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz, is the consideration of general parametrized families of unitaries rather than only those corresponding to the time-evolution under a fixed local Hamiltonian for a time specified by the parameter. This ansatz supports the representation of a larger, and potentially more useful, set of states than the original formulation, with potential long-term impact on a broad array of application areas.\nL19: For cases that call for mixing only within a desired subspace, refocusing on unitaries rather than Hamiltonians enables more efficiently implementable mixers than was possible in the original framework. Such mixers are particularly useful for optimization problems with hard constraints that must always be satisfied, defining a feasible subspace, and soft constraints whose violation we wish to minimize.\nL20: More efficient implementation enables earlier experimental exploration of an alternating operator approach to a wide variety of approximate optimization, exact optimization, and sampling problems. Here, we introduce the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz, lay out design criteria for mixing operators, detail mappings for eight problems, and provide brief descriptions of mappings for diverse problems.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2006.00354] Grover Mixers for QAOA: Shifting Complexity from Mixer Design to State Preparation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.00354)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.00354\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 30 May 2020 (【5†v1】), last revised 2 Oct 2020 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Grover Mixers for QAOA: Shifting Complexity from Mixer Design to State Preparation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Andreas Bärtschi】, 【7†Stephan Eidenbenz】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Grover Mixers for QAOA: Shifting Complexity from Mixer Design to State Preparation, by Andreas B\\\"artschi and Stephan Eidenbenz\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】\nL16: > Abstract:We propose GM-QAOA, a variation of the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz (QAOA) that uses Grover-like selective phase shift mixing operators. GM-QAOA works on any NP optimization problem for which it is possible to efficiently prepare an equal superposition of all feasible solutions; it is designed to perform particularly well for constraint optimization problems, where not all possible variable assignments are feasible solutions.\nL17: GM-QAOA has the following features: (i) It is not susceptible to Hamiltonian Simulation error (such as Trotterization errors) as its operators can be implemented exactly using standard gate sets and (ii) Solutions with the same objective value are always sampled with the same amplitude.\nL18: > We illustrate the potential of GM-QAOA on several optimization problem classes: for permutation-based optimization problems such as the Traveling Salesperson Problem, we present an efficient algorithm to prepare a superposition of all possible permutations of $n$ numbers, defined on $O(n^2)$ qubits; for the hard constraint $k$-Vertex-Cover problem, and for an application to Discrete Portfolio Rebalancing, we show that GM-QAOA outperforms existing QAOA approaches.\nL19: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph); Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)\nL20: Report number:  | LA-UR-20-23893\nL21: Cite as:  | 【9†arXiv:2006.00354】 [quant-ph]\nL22:    | (or 【10†arXiv:2006.00354v2】 [quant-ph] for this version)\nL23:    | 【11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2006.00354†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Journal reference:  | IEEE International Conference on Quantum Computing and Engineering, QCE'20, 72-82, 2020\nL25: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【12†https://doi.org/10.1109/QCE49297.2020.00020†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Andreas Bärtschi [【13†view email】]\nL29: 【5†[v1]】 Sat, 30 May 2020 20:24:53 UTC (114 KB)\nL30: [v2] Fri, 2 Oct 2020 21:02:15 UTC (116 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled Grover Mixers for QAOA: Shifting Complexity from Mixer Design to State Preparation, by Andreas B\\\"artschi and Stephan Eidenbenz\nL37: \nL38:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL39:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL40: \nL41: 【15†view license】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2304.09629] Quantum-Assisted Solution Paths for the Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem (https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09629)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09629\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 19 Apr 2023 (【5†v1】), last revised 7 May 2023 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Quantum-Assisted Solution Paths for the Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Lilly Palackal】, 【7†Benedikt Poggel】, 【8†Matthias Wulff】, 【9†Hans Ehm】, 【10†Jeanette Miriam Lorenz】, 【11†Christian B. Mendl】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Quantum-Assisted Solution Paths for the Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem, by Lilly Palackal and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【12†View PDF】 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Many relevant problems in industrial settings result in NP-hard optimization problems, such as the Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem (CVRP) or its reduced variant, the Travelling Salesperson Problem (TSP). Even with today's most powerful classical algorithms, the CVRP is challenging to solve classically.\nL17: Quantum computing may offer a way to improve the time to solution, although the question remains open as to whether Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) devices can achieve a practical advantage compared to classical heuristics. The most prominent algorithms proposed to solve combinatorial optimization problems in the NISQ era are the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) and the more general Variational Quantum Eigensolver (VQE).\nL18: However, implementing them in a way that reliably provides high-quality solutions is challenging, even for toy examples. In this work, we discuss decomposition and formulation aspects of the CVRP and propose an application-driven way to measure solution quality. Considering current hardware constraints, we reduce the CVRP to a clustering phase and a set of TSPs.\nL19: For the TSP, we extensively test both QAOA and VQE and investigate the influence of various hyperparameters, such as the classical optimizer choice and strength of constraint penalization. Results of QAOA are generally of limited quality because the algorithm does not reach the energy threshold for feasible TSP solutions, even when considering various extensions such as recursive, warm-start and constraint-preserving mixer QAOA.\nL20: On the other hand, the VQE reaches the energy threshold and shows a better performance. Our work outlines the obstacles to quantum-assisted solutions for real-world optimization problems and proposes perspectives on how to overcome them.\nL21: Comments:  | Submitted to the IEEE for possible publication\nL22: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph)\nL23: Cite as:  | 【14†arXiv:2304.09629】 [quant-ph]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2204.11890] Simulating key properties of lithium-ion batteries with a fault-tolerant quantum computer (https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11890)\nciteturn1view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11890\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 25 Apr 2022 (【5†v1】), last revised 6 Feb 2023 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Simulating key properties of lithium-ion batteries with a fault-tolerant quantum computer\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Alain Delgado】, 【7†Pablo A. M. Casares】, 【8†Roberto dos Reis】, 【9†Modjtaba Shokrian Zini】, 【10†Roberto Campos】, 【11†Norge Cruz-Hernández】, 【12†Arne-Christian Voigt】, 【13†Angus Lowe】, 【14†Soran Jahangiri】, 【15†M. A. Martin-Delgado】, 【16†Jonathan E. Mueller】, 【17†Juan Miguel Arrazola】\nL12: View a PDF of the paper titled Simulating key properties of lithium-ion batteries with a fault-tolerant quantum computer, by Alain Delgado and 11 other authors\nL13: \nL14: 【18†View PDF】 【19†HTML (experimental)】\nL15: > Abstract:There is a pressing need to develop new rechargeable battery technologies that can offer higher energy storage, faster charging, and lower costs. Despite the success of existing methods for the simulation of battery materials, they can sometimes fall short of delivering accurate and reliable results. Quantum computing has been discussed as an avenue to overcome these issues, but only limited work has been done to outline how they may impact battery simulations.\nL16: In this work, we provide a detailed answer to the following question: how can a quantum computer be used to simulate key properties of a lithium-ion battery? Based on recently-introduced first-quantization techniques, we lay out an end-to-end quantum algorithm for calculating equilibrium cell voltages, ionic mobility, and thermal stability.\nL17: These can be obtained from ground-state energies of materials, which is the core calculation executed by the quantum computer using qubitization-based quantum phase estimation. The algorithm includes explicit methods for preparing approximate ground states of periodic materials in first quantization. We bring these insights together to perform the first estimation of the resources required to implement a quantum algorithm for simulating a realistic cathode material, dilithium iron silicate.\nL18: Comments:  | 31 pages, 14 figures\nL19: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph); Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)\nL20: Cite as:  | 【20†arXiv:2204.11890】 [quant-ph]\nL21:    | (or 【21†arXiv:2204.11890v2】 [quant-ph] for this version)\nL22:    | 【22†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2204.11890†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: Journal reference:  | Phys. Rev. A 106, 032428 (2022)\nL24: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【23†https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.106.032428†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn1view5 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-17126-3\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-17126-3: (500) Redirected to https://idp.nature.com/authorize?response_type=cookie&client_id=grover&redirect_uri=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-17126-3 which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1503.00673] What Is an Emerging Technology? (https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.00673)\nciteturn1view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.00673\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 171\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Other Computer Science\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Feb 2015 (【5†v1】), last revised 4 Jan 2016 (this version, v4)]\nL9: # Title:What Is an Emerging Technology?\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Daniele Rotolo】, 【7†Diana Hicks】, 【8†Ben R. Martin】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled What Is an Emerging Technology?, by Daniele Rotolo and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【9†View PDF】 【10†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:There is considerable and growing interest in the emergence of novel technologies, especially from the policy-making perspective. Yet as an area of study, emerging technologies lacks key foundational elements, namely a consensus on what classifies a technology as 'emergent' and strong research designs that operationalize central theoretical concepts.\nL17: The present paper aims to fill this gap by developing a definition of 'emerging technologies' and linking this conceptual effort with the development of a framework for the operationalisation of technological emergence. The definition is developed by combining a basic understanding of the term and in particular the concept of 'emergence' with a review of key innovation studies dealing with definitional issues of technological emergence.\nL18: The resulting definition identifies five attributes that feature in the emergence of novel technologies. These are: (i) radical novelty, (ii) relatively fast growth, (iii) coherence, (iv) prominent impact, and (v) uncertainty and ambiguity. The framework for operationalising emerging technologies is then elaborated on the basis of the proposed attributes.\nL19: To do so, we identify and review major empirical approaches (mainly in, although not limited to, the scientometric domain) for the detection and study of emerging technologies (these include indicators and trend analysis, citation analysis, co-word analysis, overlay mapping, and combinations thereof) and elaborate on how these can be used to operationalise the different attributes of emergence.\nL20: Comments:  | Research Policy (in press)\nL21: Subjects:  | Other Computer Science (cs.OH)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【11†arXiv:1503.00673】 [cs.OH]\nL23:    | (or 【12†arXiv:1503.00673v4】 [cs.OH] for this version)\nL24:    | 【13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1503.00673†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Daniele Rotolo [【14†view email】]\nL28: 【5†[v1]】 Fri, 13 Feb 2015 17:28:50 UTC (91 KB)\nL29: 【15†[v2]】 Wed, 1 Jul 2015 13:21:38 UTC (73 KB)\nL30: 【16†[v3]】 Tue, 7 Jul 2015 09:33:47 UTC (82 KB)\nL31: [v4] Mon, 4 Jan 2016 13:16:10 UTC (82 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37